1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:03,660
Two storms, one ocean. Warn people with numbers,

2
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:07,460
fifteen north, sixty-two west, and nobody remembers.

3
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:10,300
So forecasters give storms short, human names.

4
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:13,050
Easy to say, and hard to mix up.

5
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,100
Long ago, storms were named after saints' days,

6
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:17,450
like San Felipe.

7
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:18,860
In the nineteen fifties,

8
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:22,380
forecasters used a radio alphabet. Able, Baker,

9
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:23,250
Charlie.

10
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:26,480
Then women's names, until nineteen seventy-nine,

11
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:28,550
when men's names joined in.

12
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,060
Today, six lists of names take turns,

13
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:32,940
repeating every six years.

14
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:37,670
But if a storm is truly deadly, its name is retired.

15
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,500
There will never be another Katrina.
